update public.moo_versions set body_md = body_md || E'The CDRRMO shall develop a Secretariat SOP and use standardised templates. This responds to A1.4, which is already rated 4, while addressing the need to document the existing practice more systematically.\n\n### 7.3 Process 3: Institutional Policy and Ordinance Development\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure that local DRRM policies are legally sound, risk-informed, coordinated and implementable.\n\nThe process shall be used for the proposed amendment of the CDRRMO ordinance (Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014, as amended), updated CDRRMC Executive Order, EOC establishment issuance, ICS institutionalisation and amendments to existing DRRM ordinances.\n\n**Figure 1.9**\n\n*Technical and Legal Validation of Local DRRM Policies and Ordinances*\n\n![Figure 14: Technical and Legal Validation of Local DRRM Policies and Ordinances](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-14.webp)\n\n*Note. Source: MODE platform image library (public/library/ch1v12), prepared for this chapter. Conceptual illustration; the chapter text governs.*\n\n**Workflow GOV-03: Policy Development and Approval**\n\n1.  **Identify the policy gap.** CDRRMO documents the legal, institutional or operational deficiency.\n\n2.  **Develop the technical proposal**. Prepare the proposed policy, institutional implications and implementation requirements.\n\n3.  **Conduct consultation.** Engage affected City offices, CDRRMC members, CSOs and relevant partners.\n\n4.  **Legal review.** The City Legal Office reviews legality, authority, consistency and enforceability.\n\n5.  **Resource review**. HRMO and Budget Office assess staffing, funding and administrative implications.\n\n6.  **Approva**l. Submit the proposal to the Mayor or Sangguniang Panlungsod, depending on the nature of the instrument.\n\n7.  **Publication and dissemination**. Circulate the approved policy to responsible offices and affected stakeholders.\n\n8.  **Implementation and evaluation**. Monitor compliance, assess effectiveness and recommend revisions.\n\n**Decision rule**: The appropriate approving authority shall be determined by the legal nature of the proposed instrument. An ordinance shall not be substituted by an Executive Order where legislative action is required.\n\n### 7.4 Process 4: State of Calamity Declaration\n\nThe City shall maintain a documented protocol consistent with Section 16 of RA 10121 and its IRR.\n\n**Practice to date (IAT v1.7, A1.6).** The SP adopted a State of Calamity for Typhoon Odette (SP Res. No. 2021-366, December 2021). The CDRRMC recommended a State of Calamity for the 2024 dengue outbreak for a period not exceeding one year, liftable earlier on the Council’s recommendation (CDRRMC Res. No. 06 s.2024). These cases show the process in use. They also show the gaps this protocol closes: no written criteria, no standard recommendation package, and Quick Response Fund resolutions that do not cite the declaration relied on.\n\nA State of Calamity declaration is based on the results of damage assessment and needs analysis. It shall be distinguished from the anticipatory declaration mechanism under RA 12287.\n\n**Workflow GOV-04: State of Calamity**\n\n1.  **Incident assessment**\n    > \\> BDRRMCs, response agencies and the CDRRMO report casualties, affected populations, infrastructure damage, livelihood disruption and service interruptions.\n\n2.  **RDANA consolidation**\n    > \\> CDRRMO coordinates the validation and consolidation of damage and needs information.\n\n3.  \\*\\*Technical recommendation\n    > \\>\\*\\* CDRRMO prepares the assessment and submits it to the CDRRMC.\n\n4.  **CDRRMC deliberation**\n    > \\> The Council evaluates the evidence and adopts a recommendation where the legal criteria are met.\n\n5.  \\*\\*Legislative declaration\n    > \\>\\*\\* The Sangguniang Panlungsod considers and issues the declaration or lifting through the appropriate resolution.\n\n6.  \\*\\*Implementation\n    > \\>\\*\\* The City Government implements applicable remedial measures, resource arrangements, assistance and reporting requirements.\n\n7.  \\*\\*Monitoring and lifting\n    > \\>\\*\\* The CDRRMO monitors continuing impacts and prepares the technical basis for recommending lifting when appropriate.\n\n**Required Outputs**\n\nRDANA, CDRRMC recommendation, Sangguniang Panlungsod resolution, implementation records and lifting documentation.\n\nThe City shall maintain the following supporting records:\n\n- Technical assessment and recommendation;\n\n- RDANA and other supporting reports;\n\n- CDRRMC resolution;\n\n- Sangguniang Panlungsod resolution;\n\n- Post-declaration action register;\n\n- Resource utilisation and accountability records; and\n\n- Lifting recommendation and corresponding resolution.\n\nThe protocol shall also address coordination where national or regional declarations, infrastructure closures or emergency restrictions affect Ormoc City without automatically constituting a local State of Calamity declaration.\n\nEvery resolution that uses the Quick Response Fund shall cite the declaration relied on: the SP resolution number and date, or the Proclamation number and date for a national or regional declaration. The reading of the QRF trigger shall be validated with the City Legal Office and COA.\n\n### 7.5 Process 5: State of Imminent Disaster Declaration and Anticipatory Action\n\n**New statutory Governance Process**\n\nRA 12287 establishes a mechanism for declaring a State of Imminent Disaster before a disaster occurs, based on the applicable forecast, pre-disaster risk assessment and legal criteria.\n\nThe IRR, signed on 14 September 2026 and effective upon publication and filing with the UP Office of the National Administrative Register, provides the implementing framework for this mechanism. The White, Blue and Red EOC levels used with it come from the National Disaster Response Plan as restated in RDRRMC VIII Resolution No. 12-006, s. 2023; NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018 governs EOC activation and functions.\n\nThe assessment identifies A1.8 as a mandatory requirement with a score of 1. The City has no localised procedure, request template or Mayor’s Executive Order template documented in the evidence reviewed.\n\nThe following workflow shall therefore be institutionalised.\n\n**Figure 1.10**\n\n*Anticipatory Action Pathway Under RA 12287*\n\n![Figure 15: Anticipatory Action Pathway Under RA 12287](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-15.webp)\n\n*Note. Source: MODE platform image library (public/library/ch1v12), prepared for this chapter. Conceptual illustration; the chapter text governs. The funding limits shown follow RA 12287 and its IRR; the IRR text governs.*\n\n**Workflow GOV-05: State of Imminent Disaster**\n\n1.  \\*\\*Hazard forecast and trigger monitoring\n    > \\>\\*\\* CDRRMO Operations and Warning monitors official forecasts, advisories and hazard-specific triggers from PAGASA, PHIVOLCS and other competent agencies.\n\n2.  \\*\\*Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment\n    > \\>\\*\\* CDRRMO coordinates the assessment of exposure, vulnerability, projected severity, likely impacts, affected sectors, lead time and available response capacity.\n\n3.  **Technical recommendation**\n    > \\> CDRRMO prepares the PDRA findings and proposed anticipatory measures for CDRRMC consideration.\n\n4.  \\*\\*Regional coordination and recommendation\n    > \\>\\*\\* The City coordinates with RDRRMC VIII through OCD Region VIII and submits the prescribed request and supporting documentation.\n\n5.  **Regional decision and response**\n    > \\> RDRRMC VIII undertakes the action required under the Act and IRR. The request, acknowledgment and prescribed response timeline are documented.\n\n6.  **Local declaration**\n    > \\> Upon the applicable recommendation, the City Mayor may declare a State of Imminent Disaster through an Executive Order covering the jurisdiction forecast to be affected.\n\n7.  **Activation of anticipatory measures**\n    > \\> Authorised offices implement approved early actions, including pre-positioning, evacuation, protection of critical infrastructure and targeted social assistance.\n\n8.  \\*\\*Funding and accountability\n    > \\>\\*\\* Budget, Accounting, Procurement and implementing offices document commitments, disbursements, utilisation and outputs under the IRR and applicable DBM-COA rules.\n\n9.  \\*\\*Lifting and post-declaration reporting\n    > \\>\\*\\* The City observes the applicable lifting procedures and submits the required post-lifting report to OCD within the prescribed 45-day period.\n\nThe process shall observe the following safeguards:\n\n1.  A forecast alone shall not automatically trigger a declaration. The applicable legal criteria and PDRA requirements must be met.\n\n2.  The CDRRMO shall provide technical recommendations, not independently exercise the declaration authority.\n\n3.  The local declaration shall follow the recommendation and coordination mechanism prescribed by RA 12287 and its IRR.\n\n4.  Anticipatory actions shall be proportionate to the projected impacts, hazard lead time, available resources and applicable legal authority.\n\n5.  Measures shall observe inclusion, protection, do-no-harm and no-regrets principles.\n\n6.  The use of funds shall follow the statutory funding limits, tranche arrangements and applicable financial regulations.\n\n7.  Decisions, approvals, actions and expenditures shall be documented for audit and evaluation.\n\n**Funding control**: As reflected in the assessment workbook, the IRR provides for an anticipatory-action allocation of up to 10% of the 70% LDRRMF component and a 30%/70% tranche arrangement associated with the prescribed three-day lead-time rule. The precise timing, eligibility, disbursement and documentation requirements must be implemented using the certified IRR and applicable DBM-COA guidance. The MOO shall not treat this allocation as an additional fund outside the statutory LDRRMF framework.\n\n**Independent Component City clarification:** Ormoc’s status as an Independent Component City must be considered when establishing its vertical coordination arrangements. The City shall obtain written confirmation from OCD Region VIII on the application of the IRR’s regional and provincial coordination provisions, particularly whether any provincial declaration has legal applicability to Ormoc. The City’s ICC status shall not be used as a reason to bypass the RDRRMC recommendation mechanism expressly established by RA 12287.\n\n### 7.6 Process 6: Institutional Escalation and Inter-Agency Coordination\n\nInstitutional escalation shall occur when:\n\n- The scale of an incident exceeds the capacity of a barangay;\n\n- Multiple barangays require coordinated intervention;\n\n- City resources are insufficient;\n\n- Specialised national or regional assistance is required;\n\n- Critical infrastructure or essential services are disrupted; or\n\n- A statutory declaration or executive decision is necessary.\n\nRA 10121, Section 15, provides the general coordination framework: barangay-level leadership where a barangay is affected, city/municipal leadership where two or more barangays are affected, and progressively higher coordination levels when impacts exceed the relevant territorial scale. Higher councils and institutions support the LGU’s primary response responsibility.\n\n**Figure 1.11**\n\n*Escalation From Barangay to City to Regional and National Coordination*\n\n![Figure 16: Escalation From Barangay to City to Regional and National Coordination](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-16.webp)\n\n*Note. Source: MODE platform image library (public/library/ch1v12), prepared for this chapter. Conceptual illustration; the chapter text governs. The ring boundaries are illustrative. Activation triggers come from the approved EOC executive order and contingency plans, not from this figure.*\n\nThe operational workflow shall be:\n\n1.  Incident or risk information is reported to the appropriate local authority.\n\n2.  The affected BDRRMC undertakes initial action and reports to the City.\n\n3.  CDRRMO validates the information and assesses the level of support required.\n\n4.  The CDRRMC and EOC coordinate City-wide institutional action, as appropriate.\n\n5.  The City requests external assistance through the prescribed channels when required.\n\n6.  The receiving institution acknowledges and assigns the request.\n\n7.  The CDRRMO monitors the response and maintains the institutional record.\n\n8.  Unresolved requirements are escalated through the appropriate higher-level coordination mechanism.\n\n### 7.7 Process 7: Governance Review and Corrective Action\n\nAll governance processes shall be subject to periodic review.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall:\n\n1.  Consolidate compliance and performance information;\n\n2.  Compare actual results against approved targets;\n\n3.  Identify gaps, recurring problems and systemic causes;\n\n4.  Assign corrective actions to accountable offices;\n\n5.  Establish completion dates and required resources;\n\n6.  Verify implementation;\n\n7.  Report unresolved issues to the CDRRMC; and\n\n8.  Update policies and procedures when necessary.\n\nThis process shall link the Institutional Assessment Tool, Action Plan, CDRRMC action register, annual LDRRMP monitoring and the MOO revision mechanism.\n\n**Figure 1.12**\n\n*Governance Review: From Findings to Verified Corrective Action*\n\n![Figure 17: Governance Review: From Findings to Verified Corrective Action](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-17.webp)\n\n*Note. Source: MODE platform image library (public/library/ch1v12), prepared for this chapter. Conceptual illustration; the chapter text governs.*\n\n## 8. Responsibility Assignment and RACI Framework\n\n| **\\[R\\] Responsible** | **\\[A\\] Accountable** | **\\[C\\] Consulted** | **\\[I\\] Informed** |\n|-----------------------|-----------------------|---------------------|--------------------|\n\n### 8.1 Application\n\nThe RACI framework shall be used to clarify institutional responsibility for governance processes and prevent overlapping mandates, unassigned tasks and ambiguous decision authority.\n\nThe following definitions apply:\n\n**Table 1.14**\n\n*Application*\n\n| **Code** | **Responsibility** | **Application**                                                               |\n|----------|--------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| R        | Responsible        | Performs the work and produces the required output.                           |\n| A        | Accountable        | Owns the result and approves the output or decision. Only one A per activity. |\n| C        | Consulted          | Provides technical, legal or operational advice before the decision.          |\n| I        | Informed           | Receives relevant information on the decision or outcome.                     |\n\nRACI assignments shall be interpreted subject to statutory powers, formal delegations and approved organisational arrangements. A RACI designation does not create authority that does not otherwise exist under law.\n\n### 8.2 Institutional Roles Used in the RACI Matrix\n\n| **\\[R\\] Responsible** | **\\[A\\] Accountable** | **\\[C\\] Consulted** | **\\[I\\] Informed** |\n|-----------------------|-----------------------|---------------------|--------------------|\n\n**Table 1.15**\n\n*Institutional Roles Used in the RACI Matrix*\n\n| **Role**                             | **Abbreviation** | **Principal Responsibility**                                                        |\n|--------------------------------------|------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Local Chief Executive                | Mayor            | Executive authority and statutory decisions.                                        |\n| Sangguniang Panlungsod               | SP               | Legislative approval and local declaration authority.                               |\n| CDRRMC                               | Council          | Collective policy direction, deliberation and recommendation.                       |\n| CDRRMO Head                          | CDRRMO           | Permanent office leadership and technical accountability.                           |\n| CDRRMO Secretariat                   | Secretariat      | Council administration, documentation and action tracking.                          |\n| City Legal Office                    | CLO              | Legal review and compliance advice.                                                 |\n| City Planning and Development Office | CPDO             | Integration of DRR/CCA into development planning.                                   |\n| HRMO                                 | HRMO             | Staffing, qualification and personnel administration.                               |\n| City Budget / Accounting / BAC       | Finance          | Budgeting, accounting, procurement and financial control.                           |\n| City Information Office              | CIO              | Public information and risk communication support.                                  |\n| BDRRMCs                              | Barangays        | Community-level governance, reporting and local action.                             |\n| City departments and agencies        | Departments      | Sectoral implementation and technical support.                                      |\n| OCD / RDRRMC VIII                    | Regional         | Regional coordination, technical assistance and statutory recommendation functions. |\n| EOC Manager / IMT                    | EOC/IMT          | Emergency coordination and incident-management interface.                           |\n| CSOs / Private Sector                | Partners         | Community participation, technical support and resource commitments.                |\n\n### 8.3 Master RACI Matrix – Institutional Governance\n\n| **\\[R\\] Responsible** | **\\[A\\] Accountable** | **\\[C\\] Consulted** | **\\[I\\] Informed** |\n|-----------------------|-----------------------|---------------------|--------------------|\n\nThe following matrix establishes the proposed responsibility assignments for Chapter 1.\n\n**Table 1.16**\n\n*Master RACI Matrix – Institutional Governance*\n\n| **Governance Activity**                                   | **Mayor** | **SP** | **CDRRMC** | **CDRRMO** | **Secretariat** | **CLO** | **HRMO / Finance** | **OCD VIII** |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------|------------|------------|-----------------|---------|--------------------|--------------|\n| Establish CDRRMO through ordinance                        | I         | A      | C          | R          | I               | C       | C                  | I            |\n| Prepare CDRRMC composition EO                             | A         | I      | C          | R          | R               | C       | I                  | I            |\n| Maintain CDRRMC membership register                       | I         | I      | C          | A          | R               | C       | I                  | I            |\n| Prepare CDRRMC agenda and meetings                        | I         | I      | A          | C          | R               | C       | I                  | I            |\n| Document Council resolutions                              | I         | I      | A          | C          | R               | C       | I                  | I            |\n| Monitor Council decisions                                 | I         | I      | A          | R          | R               | I       | C                  | I            |\n| Develop local DRRM policy                                 | A         | C      | C          | R          | C               | C       | C                  | I            |\n| Review proposed DRRM ordinances                           | C         | I      | C          | R          | I               | A       | C                  | C            |\n| Prepare State of Calamity technical assessment            | I         | I      | C          | A          | R               | C       | C                  | I            |\n| Recommend State of Calamity                               | I         | I      | A          | R          | R               | C       | I                  | I            |\n| Issue local State of Calamity declaration                 | I         | A      | R          | C          | I               | C       | I                  | I            |\n| Prepare State of Imminent Disaster PDRA                   | I         | I      | C          | A          | I               | C       | C                  | C            |\n| Submit request to RDRRMC VIII                             | I         | I      | A          | R          | C               | C       | I                  | C            |\n| Recommend regional State of Imminent Disaster Declaration | I         | I      | C          | C          | I               | I       | I                  | A/R\\*        |\n| Issue local State of Imminent Disaster EO                 | A         | I      | C          | R          | I               | C       | C                  | C            |\n| Coordinate anticipatory action                            | A         | I      | C          | R          | I               | C       | C                  | C            |\n| Prepare 45-day post-lifting report                        | I         | I      | C          | A/R        | C               | C       | R                  | I            |\n| Maintain legal and policy register                        | I         | I      | I          | A          | R               | C       | I                  | I            |\n| Conduct annual governance review                          | I         | I      | A          | R          | R               | C       | C                  | C            |\n\n\\*The RACI assignment for OCD/RDRRMC VIII reflects the regional recommendation function described in RA 12287. The exact responsible regional body and administrative sequence shall be finalised against the certified IRR and applicable regional procedures. The RACI matrix does not replace the statutory decision authority of the NDRRMC, regional councils, Mayor or Sanggunian.\n\n### 8.4 RACI Matrix – Institutional Interfaces\n\nPart C of the assessment shall be operationalised through the following responsibility arrangements.\n\n**Table 1.17**\n\n*RACI Matrix – Institutional Interfaces*\n\n| **Interface Activity**                  | **Accountable Owner**                        | **Responsible Actors**             | **CDRRMO Role**                                 |\n|-----------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| CDRA and CLUP integration               | CPDO                                         | CPDO technical team                | C / technical input                             |\n| DRR/CCA project screening               | CPDO                                         | CPDO, City Budget Office           | C / risk validation                             |\n| Critical facility structural assessment | City Engineer / Building Official            | Engineering and inspection teams   | C / risk prioritisation                         |\n| Utility continuity agreements           | Relevant utility / designated City authority | Utility providers and City offices | R for coordination, subject to approved mandate |\n| Watershed and ecosystem monitoring      | CENRO                                        | CENRO, DENR and partners           | C / DRRM integration                            |\n| Health anticipatory action              | City Health Officer                          | CHO, hospitals and health partners | C / coordination                                |\n| Education anticipatory action           | DepEd Schools Division                       | School DRRM coordinators           | C / coordination                                |\n| Agriculture and fisheries early action  | City Agriculture / Veterinary Office         | CAO, CVO and relevant agencies     | C / coordination                                |\n| Price monitoring                        | DTI / City Market Office                     | Market monitoring personnel        | C / EOC information integration                 |\n| Business continuity outreach            | CDRRMO Head                                  | CDRRMO and Chamber of Commerce     | A / R for DRRM outreach                         |\n\nThis matrix ensures that the CDRRMO coordinates the overall resilience system without appropriating the mandates of other City offices.\n\n### 8.5 RACI Compliance Rules\n\nThe following rules shall govern all RACI matrices in the MOO:\n\n1.  Each activity shall have one clearly designated Accountable role.\n\n2.  Multiple Responsible roles may be assigned where work is genuinely shared.\n\n3.  Consulted roles shall be limited to those whose technical or institutional input is necessary.\n\n4.  Informed roles shall receive the relevant outputs without becoming unnecessary approval points.\n\n5.  RACI assignments shall be reviewed whenever the organisational structure, legal mandate or delegation changes.\n\n6.  Where a statutory body exercises collective authority, the Council or Sanggunian shall be identified as the accountable decision-making body, with the designated Chairperson responsible for convening and presiding over proceedings.\n\n7.  Each RACI matrix shall identify the approving authority for the final output.\n\nThe detailed job action sheets and position-level RACI matrices shall be developed under the organisational and EOC modules.\n\n## 9. Total Quality Management and Institutional Performance\n\n### 9.1 Quality Management Approach\n\nThe CDRRMO shall apply Total Quality Management principles to the design, implementation, monitoring and improvement of its governance processes.\n\nTQM shall be treated as an institutional management approach rather than a separate administrative activity. Its purpose is to ensure that DRRM services are reliable, timely, accountable, evidence-based and responsive to the needs of affected communities.\n\n**Figure 1.13**\n\n*Total Quality Management Applied to DRRM Governance*\n\n![Figure 18: Total Quality Management Applied to DRRM Governance](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-18.webp)\n\n*Note. Source: MODE platform image library (public/library/ch1v12), prepared for this chapter. Conceptual illustration; the chapter text governs.*\n\nThe following principles shall guide implementation:\n\n**Table 1.18**\n\n*Quality Management Approach*\n\n| **TQM Principle**              | **Application to CDRRMO–EOC Operations**                                                                                       |\n|--------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Customer and stakeholder focus | Decisions and services shall respond to the needs of communities, affected populations, responders and institutional partners. |\n| Leadership and accountability  | Each process shall have a designated owner and authorised decision-maker.                                                      |\n| Process orientation            | Activities shall be standardised through workflows, SOPs, checklists and defined outputs.                                      |\n| Systems approach               | Institutional processes shall be managed as interconnected functions rather than isolated activities.                          |\n| Evidence-based decisions       | Decisions shall be supported by verified risk information, assessment results, operational reports and documented evidence.    |\n| People engagement              | Personnel, barangays, CSOs and partners shall participate in process design, implementation and improvement.                   |\n| Continuous improvement         | Findings from exercises, incidents, audits and assessments shall result in corrective and preventive action.                   |\n| Relationship management        | Partnerships and institutional interfaces shall be actively maintained and evaluated.                                          |\n\n### 9.2 Plan–Do–Check–Act (PDCA) Cycle\n\nThe City shall apply the PDCA cycle as the primary mechanism for institutional quality improvement.\n\nThe PDCA cycle shall operate continuously across all CDRRMO and EOC functional modules.\n\n### 9.3 Application of PDCA to Institutional Governance\n\n**Table 1.19**\n\n*Application of PDCA to Institutional Governance*\n\n| **PDCA Stage** | **Key Activities**                                                                                    | **Responsible Institutional Actors**              | **Required Evidence**                                     |\n|----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|\n| Plan           | Annual governance calendar, policy review, risk-informed priorities, budget and staffing requirements | CDRRMO Head, CDRRMC, City Planning, Budget Office | Approved plans, meeting calendar, LDRRMFIP                |\n| Do             | Meetings, policy implementation, coordination, declaration procedures and institutional operations    | CDRRMO, Secretariat, City offices and partners    | Minutes, resolutions, SOP records, reports                |\n| Check          | Compliance assessment, performance monitoring, document verification and review of decisions          | CDRRMO, Internal Audit, CDRRMC                    | Dashboard, audit reports, compliance register             |\n| Act            | Corrective action, institutional restructuring, policy amendment and procedure revision               | Mayor, CDRRMC, SP and responsible offices         | Corrective action register, revised policies, updated MOO |\n\n### 9.4 Process Performance Indicators\n\nThe following indicators are proposed for Chapter 1. They shall be integrated into the broader MOO Monitoring and Evaluation Framework.\n\n**Table 1.20**\n\n*Process Performance Indicators*\n\n| **Performance Indicator**                                                          | **Proposed Target**                        | **Frequency**        | **Process Owner**  |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| CDRRMC regular meetings conducted                                                  | At least 4 annually                        | Quarterly            | CDRRMC Secretariat |\n| CDRRMC membership register updated                                                 | 100% of prescribed seats documented        | Annual               | CDRRMO             |\n| CDRRMC resolutions with assigned owners and deadlines                              | 100%                                       | Every meeting        | Secretariat        |\n| CDRRMC action items with documented status                                         | 100% tracked                               | Quarterly            | CDRRMO             |\n| Critical DRRM policies subjected to legal review                                   | 100% before approval                       | Per policy           | City Legal Office  |\n| Critical governance procedures with approved SOPs                                  | 100% of identified mandatory procedures    | Annual review        | CDRRMO             |\n| State of Calamity recommendations with supporting RDANA                            | 100%                                       | Per occurrence       | CDRRMO             |\n| State of Imminent Disaster requests with complete PDRA documentation               | 100%                                       | Per applicable event | CDRRMO             |\n| State of Imminent Disaster post-lifting reports submitted within prescribed period | 100%                                       | Per declaration      | CDRRMO             |\n| CDRRMC decisions with documented follow-through                                    | 100% recorded                              | Quarterly            | CDRRMO Secretariat |\n| High-priority institutional corrective actions completed by approved deadlines     | At least 90%, with overdue items escalated | Quarterly            | CDRRMO Head        |\n\nThese are proposed management targets, not existing performance claims. They shall be calibrated during the validation workshop and approved through the City’s institutional performance arrangements.\n\n### 9.5 Corrective and Preventive Action\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a Corrective and Preventive Action Register (CAPA) linked to:\n\n- Institutional Assessment Tool findings;\n\n- CDRRMC resolutions;\n\n- Internal and external audit findings;\n\n- After-action reviews;\n\n- Exercise evaluation reports;\n\n- Incident and response reports;\n\n- Stakeholder feedback; and\n\n- Applicable changes in laws and policies.\n\nEach corrective action shall identify:\n\n**Table 1.21**\n\n*Corrective and Preventive Action*\n\n| **CAPA Field**       | **Required Information**                                                                   |\n|----------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Reference            | Source of the finding or non-conformity                                                    |\n| Problem statement    | Specific deficiency or process failure                                                     |\n| Root cause           | Underlying organisational, procedural, technical or resource-related cause                 |\n| Risk consequence     | Potential effect on life safety, service delivery, compliance or institutional performance |\n| Corrective action    | Specific intervention to address the deficiency                                            |\n| Accountable owner    | Single office or official responsible for completion                                       |\n| Responsible actors   | Personnel or offices undertaking the work                                                  |\n| Target date          | Approved completion deadline                                                               |\n| Required resources   | Budget, personnel, equipment or technical assistance                                       |\n| Verification         | Evidence that the action was implemented                                                   |\n| Effectiveness review | Evidence that the original problem has been resolved and has not recurred                  |\n\nA finding shall not be closed merely because an action has been initiated. Closure requires evidence of completion and verification of effectiveness.\n\n## 10. Alignment With the MCR2030 Ten Essentials\n\nThis chapter primarily contributes to the Governance and Financial Capacity cluster of the MCR2030 Ten Essentials while establishing the institutional foundation for the other two clusters.\n\n**Figure 1.14**\n\n*Alignment of Chapter 1 Instruments With the MCR2030 Ten Essentials*\n\n![Figure 19: Alignment of Chapter 1 Instruments With the MCR2030 Ten Essentials](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-19.webp)\n\n*Note. Source: MODE platform image library (public/library/ch1v12), prepared for this chapter. Conceptual illustration; the chapter text governs.*\n\n**Table 1.22**\n\n*Alignment With the MCR2030 Ten Essentials*\n\n| **MCR2030 Essential**                                              | **Chapter 1 Contribution** | **Institutional Mechanism**                                                             |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1\\. Organise for Disaster Resilience                               | Primary                    | CDRRMC, CDRRMO, EOC mandates, authorities and coordination architecture.                |\n| 2\\. Identify, Understand and Use Current and Future Risk Scenarios | Supporting                 | Institutional responsibility for risk intelligence and evidence-based policy decisions. |\n| 3\\. Strengthen Financial Capability for Resilience                 | Supporting                 | Governance of DRRM investment, funding, accountability and resource mobilisation.       |\n| 4\\. Pursue Resilient Urban Development and Design                  | Supporting                 | Institutional interface with CPDO, Zoning Administrator and City Engineer.              |\n| 5\\. Safeguard Natural Buffers to Enhance Protective Functions      | Supporting                 | Institutional interface with CENRO, DENR and watershed partners.                        |\n| 6\\. Strengthen Institutional Capacity for Resilience               | Primary                    | Institutional roles, accountability, staffing, competence and continuity.               |\n| 7\\. Understand and Strengthen Societal Capacity for Resilience     | Primary                    | CDRRMC representation, barangay participation, CSO and private-sector partnerships.     |\n| 8\\. Increase Infrastructure Resilience                             | Supporting                 | Cross-office coordination on critical facilities and lifeline continuity.               |\n| 9\\. Ensure Effective Disaster Response                             | Primary                    | EOC mandate, coordination arrangements and institutional decision pathways.             |\n| 10\\. Expedite Recovery and Build Back Better                       | Supporting                 | Governance continuity and accountability for recovery and reconstruction.               |\n\nThe City shall use this alignment to ensure that institutional improvements contribute to the wider urban resilience agenda, not merely to the internal functionality of the CDRRMO and EOC.\n\n## 11. Institutional Improvement Priorities Arising From the Assessment\n\nThe following actions are directly linked to the assessment findings and shall be included in the Institutional Improvement Action Plan.\n\nPriority is the workbook’s gap-priority band: (4 − score) × weight, × 1.5 for mandatory items; High is 9 or more and Medium is 4 or more. Owners, targets and status follow the IAT v1.7 Action Plan.\n\n**Table 1.23**\n\n*Institutional Improvement Priorities and Action Plan Targets*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 6%" />\n<col style="width: 10%" />\n<col style="width: 39%" />\n<col style="width: 13%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 14%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Priority</strong></th>\n<th><strong>IAT Ref (Score)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Required Action</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Accountable Owner</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Supporting Actors</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Target / Status</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>High</th>\n<th>A1.1 (2)</th>\n<th>Amend Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 (as amended by No. 011, s. 2015) to establish the CDRRMO as a regular department with the JMC 2014-1 divisions; attach the Chapter 2 organogram and staffing pattern.</th>\n<th>Mayor / SP (legislative process)</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head; CLO; HRMO; SP Committee on DRRM</th>\n<th><p>Q1 2027 (SP filing)</p>\n<p>Not started</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>High</th>\n<th>A1.8 (1)</th>\n<th>Establish the State of Imminent Disaster procedure: PDRA trigger, CDRRMC resolution, request to RDRRMC VIII, Mayor''s EO template, adoption rule, lifting and the 45-day report. Test it in a tabletop exercise.</th>\n<th>Mayor (declaration); CDRRMO Head (process)</th>\n<th>CDRRMC; OCD VIII; CLO; Budget Office</th>\n<th><p>Jan 2027 (with MOP)</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>High</th>\n<th>B1.1 (2)</th>\n<th>Issue an EO establishing the City EOC under NDRRMC Memo 131 s.2018: the 24/7 OpCen becomes the EOC on activation; the EOC links clusters and the IMT.</th>\n<th>Mayor</th>\n<th>CDRRMO; CLO; CDRRMC</th>\n<th><p>Jan 2027 (with MOP)</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>High</th>\n<th>B6.1 (1)</th>\n<th>Define the ICS–EOC interface in the ICS EO: the EOC supports the Incident Commander and does not command.</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th>Mayor''s Office; CLO; response clusters</th>\n<th><p>Jan 2027 (with MOP)</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>High</th>\n<th>A2.1 (2)</th>\n<th>Adopt the JMC 2014-1 organogram (Head + 3 divisions) with separate normal-operations and EOC roles, through the A1.1 ordinance and Chapter 2.</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th>HRMO</th>\n<th><p>Jan 2027 (with MOP)</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>High</th>\n<th>B1.4 / B4.1 / B6.1 (1–2)</th>\n<th>Adopt the scenario-based EOC structure, activation levels and scenario modules (section 6.3) in the EOC EO and each contingency plan; test them in an exercise.</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th>CDRRMC; partner offices; OCD VIII</th>\n<th><p>Jan 2027 (with MOP)</p>\n<p>Not started</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>Medium</th>\n<th>A4.4 / A10.4 (2)</th>\n<th>Table the 2024–2026 LDRRMF split by thematic area; adopt the budget-cycle RACI (Chapter 2, 16.2); start a quarterly results-and-finance report.</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th>All divisions; Budget Office; CDRRMC</th>\n<th><p>Next CDRRMC meeting</p>\n<p>Not started</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>Medium</th>\n<th>A1.6 (3)</th>\n<th>Write the State of Calamity protocol and templates; require each QRF resolution to cite the declaration relied on; validate the QRF trigger with the CLO and COA.</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Research and Planning</th>\n<th>CDRRMC; CLO; RDANA team</th>\n<th><p>Jan 2027 (with MOP)</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>Low</th>\n<th>A1.2 (3)</th>\n<th>Issue an amending EO to EO 2024-032A: add the City College of Ormoc, restore an academe seat, fill the third private-sector seat and cite SP accreditation of each CSO. Review the roster yearly.</th>\n<th>Mayor</th>\n<th>CDRRMO (Secretariat); CLO</th>\n<th><p>Dec 2026</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>Low</th>\n<th>A1.3 / A1.4 (3)</th>\n<th>Adopt the Secretariat SOP and resolution register; have the Mayor designate the presiding officer in writing; correct and certify the 2025 resolutions; clean the 2021–2025 resolution and EO files.</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head (Secretariat)</th>\n<th>CDRRMC members</th>\n<th><p>Dec 2026; quarterly</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>Low</th>\n<th>A1.5 (3)</th>\n<th>Amend the evacuation ordinance (triggers linked to the warning–action matrix, authority to order, assistance to PWD and older persons, livestock and pets, enforcement); add barangay-level class and work suspension; add hazard overlay zones in the CLUP/ZO update.</th>\n<th>Mayor / SP</th>\n<th>CDRRMO; CLO; CPDO; barangays</th>\n<th><p>Q2 2027</p>\n<p>Not started</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>Low</th>\n<th>A1.7 / A4.6 (3)</th>\n<th>Use the CDC and LFC seats to secure the anticipatory-action line and the EOC budget in the FY2028 budget call; file minutes that record CDRRMO input.</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th>Budget Office; Accounting; BAC</th>\n<th><p>2027 budget call</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n*Note. Owners, targets and status follow the IAT v1.7 Action Plan; confirm them at the December 2026 validation workshop.*\n\nThe action plan should retain the detailed status, funding, target dates and evidence requirements in the workbook. This chapter establishes the governance requirements and ownership; it does not replace the Action Plan.\n\n### 11.1 Priority Sequencing\n\nThe improvement actions shall be implemented in the following sequence:\n\n**Phase 1 – Legal and institutional foundation**\n\n- Amendment of the CDRRMO ordinance (No. 008, s. 2014);\n\n- Updated CDRRMC Executive Order;\n\n- EOC establishment issuance;\n\n- State of Imminent Disaster SOP;\n\n- State of Calamity SOP.\n\n**Phase 2 – Operational institutionalisation**\n\n- Secretariat SOP and action register;\n\n- Clean-up of the CDRRMC resolution and Executive Order files for 2021–2025;\n\n- Formal inter-agency coordination and delegation arrangements;\n\n- Approved CDRRMO and EOC role descriptions;\n\n- Institutional policy and records register.\n\n**Phase 3 – Performance and continuous improvement**\n\n- CDRRMC governance performance monitoring;\n\n- CAPA implementation;\n\n- Periodic legal and policy review;\n\n- Validation and testing of institutional procedures.\n\n## 12. Required SOPs, Forms and Institutional Records\n\nThe following instruments shall be maintained under this chapter.\n\n**Table 1.24**\n\n*Required SOPs, Forms and Institutional Records*\n\n| **Code** | **SOP / Instrument**                               | **Required Output**                                      |\n|----------|----------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|\n| GOV-01   | CDRRMC governance and meeting procedure            | Annual calendar, notices, agendas and minutes            |\n| GOV-02   | CDRRMC Secretariat management                      | Secretariat register and document-control system         |\n| GOV-03   | Institutional policy and ordinance development     | Policy development and legal review checklist            |\n| GOV-04   | State of Calamity declaration and lifting          | RDANA-based recommendation and declaration package       |\n| GOV-05   | State of Imminent Disaster declaration and lifting | PDRA, regional request, Mayor’s EO and reporting package |\n| GOV-06   | Inter-agency and vertical coordination             | Coordination protocol and contact directory              |\n| GOV-07   | RACI and delegation management                     | Responsibility and authority register                    |\n| GOV-08   | Institutional records management                   | Controlled document and records register                 |\n| GOV-09   | Governance performance monitoring                  | KPI dashboard and action register                        |\n| GOV-10   | Corrective and preventive action management        | CAPA register and verification reports                   |\n\nThe following templates shall be included in the MOO annexes:\n\n- **GOV-F01 – CDRRMC Membership and Designation Register;**\n\n- **GOV-F02 – CDRRMC Meeting Notice and Agenda;**\n\n- **GOV-F03 – CDRRMC Attendance and Minutes;**\n\n- **GOV-F04 – CDRRMC Resolution Template;**\n\n- **GOV-F05 – Resolution and Action Monitoring Register;**\n\n- **GOV-F06 – Institutional Policy Review Checklist;**\n\n- **GOV-F07 – State of Calamity Technical Assessment;**\n\n- **GOV-F08 – State of Calamity Recommendation and Resolution;**\n\n- **GOV-F09 – State of Imminent Disaster PDRA Summary;**\n\n- **GOV-F10 – Request to RDRRMC VIII;**\n\n- **GOV-F11 – Mayor’s State of Imminent Disaster Executive Order Template;**\n\n- **GOV-F12 – State of Imminent Disaster Lifting Documentation;**\n\n- **GOV-F13 – Post-Lifting 45-Day Report;**\n\n- **GOV-F14 – Institutional RACI Register;**\n\n- **GOV-F15 – Corrective and Preventive Action Register;**\n\n- **GOV-F16 – Legal and Policy Register.**\n\nAll templates shall have document identifiers, version numbers, approval status, responsible custodian and review dates.\n\n## 13. Governance Controls, Documentation and Records Management\n\nThe CDRRMO shall establish a controlled documentation system to ensure that institutional decisions and procedures remain accessible, authentic, traceable and current.\n\n**Figure 1.15**\n\n*Governance Controls and the Three Tests of Assurance*\n\n![Figure 20: Governance Controls and the Three Tests of Assurance](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-20.webp)\n\n*Note. Source: MODE platform image library (public/library/ch1v12), prepared for this chapter. Conceptual illustration; the chapter text governs.*\n\n### 13.1 Document Control\n\nEach controlled document shall contain:\n\n- Document title and code;\n\n- Version and revision number;\n\n- Date of preparation;\n\n- Prepared-by and reviewed-by designations;\n\n- Approving authority;\n\n- Effective date;\n\n- Revision history; and\n\n- Distribution and access classification.\n\nOnly approved versions shall be used as official operational references.\n\n### 13.2 Records Management\n\nThe CDRRMO Secretariat shall maintain the official governance records in coordination with the City Records Officer.\n\nRecords shall be classified into:\n\n1.  Legal and policy records;\n\n2.  CDRRMC governance records;\n\n3.  Institutional organisational records;\n\n4.  Declaration and executive decision records;\n\n5.  Financial and resource accountability records;\n\n6.  Coordination and partnership records;\n\n7.  Performance and audit records; and\n\n8.  Corrective action and improvement records.\n\nRetention and disposition shall follow applicable government records management and archival requirements.\n\n### 13.3 Data Protection and Information Sharing\n\nThe CDRRMO shall coordinate with the City’s Data Protection Officer and information technology office to establish appropriate safeguards for DRRM information.\n\nThe system shall distinguish:\n\n- Publicly available risk and preparedness information;\n\n- Internal institutional information;\n\n- Restricted operational information;\n\n- Personal information of affected populations;\n\n- Sensitive personal information; and\n\n- Confidential personnel and security records.\n\nThe EOC shall use aggregated and appropriately anonymised information for operational dashboards whenever personal identifiers are not required.\n\nData sharing with CSWDO, CPDO, national agencies, barangays and partners shall be governed by appropriate data-sharing agreements and privacy safeguards under RA 10173.\n\n## 14. Implementation, Review and Institutional Assurance\n\n### 14.1 Institutional Ownership\n\nThe CDRRMO Head shall be the overall process owner for the implementation of this chapter, under the administrative authority of the Local Chief Executive and the policy oversight of the CDRRMC.\n\nThe City Legal Office, HRMO, Budget Office, Accounting Office, Secretariat and other concerned institutions shall discharge their assigned responsibilities.\n\n### 14.2 Review Schedule\n\n**Table 1.25**\n\n*Review Schedule*\n\n| **Review Activity**              | **Frequency**                                      | **Responsible Owner**      |\n|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|\n| CDRRMC membership review         | Annually and after material organisational changes | CDRRMO                     |\n| Governance SOP review            | Annually and after significant policy changes      | CDRRMO Head                |\n| Legal and policy register review | At least annually                                  | CDRRMO / City Legal Office |\n| RACI and delegation review       | Annually and after structural changes              | CDRRMO / HRMO              |\n| CDRRMC performance review        | Quarterly, with annual consolidation               | CDRRMC Secretariat         |\n| Institutional assessment         | Annually, or after major institutional changes     | CDRRMO                     |\n| CAPA review                      | Quarterly                                          | CDRRMO Head                |\n| MOO revision                     | Annually or when necessary                         | CDRRMO Head                |\n\n### 14.3 Institutional Assurance\n\nThe implementation of this chapter shall be evaluated through three complementary tests:\n\nCompliance test: Are statutory and regulatory requirements met?\n\nEffectiveness test: Do institutional arrangements produce timely, coordinated and reliable decisions and outputs?\n\nSustainability test: Are the arrangements institutionalised, documented, resourced, tested and capable of surviving leadership and personnel changes?\n\nA governance process shall be considered effective only when it satisfies all three dimensions appropriate to its function.\n\n## 15. Technical Validation Notes and Outstanding Decisions\n\nThe following matters should be resolved before Chapter 1 is submitted for formal approval.\n\n**Table 1.26**\n\n*Technical Validation Notes and Outstanding Decisions*\n\n| **Validation Issue**       | **Required Action**                                                                                                                                                             | **Responsible Institution**  |\n|----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------|\n| CDRRMO legal status        | Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 (amended by No. 011, s. 2015) creates the office. Decide whether to amend it for department status and plantilla, and update the A1.1 evidence.      | City Legal Office / SP       |\n| CDRRMC composition         | Verify all statutory seats and CSO accreditation.                                                                                                                               | CDRRMO / Mayor’s Office      |\n| State of Calamity          | Validate local declaration and lifting templates against RA 10121 Section 16.                                                                                                   | City Legal Office            |\n| State of Imminent Disaster | Confirm certified IRR provisions, RDRRMC VIII request route, response timelines and local EO procedure.                                                                         | OCD VIII / City Legal Office |\n| ICC coordination           | Confirm applicability of provincial coordination provisions to Ormoc.                                                                                                           | OCD VIII                     |\n| EOC establishment          | Verify NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018 (25 October 2018) against a certified copy; alert colours are cited to the NDRP and RDRRMC VIII Res. No. 12-006, s. 2023.             | OCD VIII                     |\n| EOC–ICS interface          | Confirm the relationship between EOC, IMT, Incident Commander and response clusters.                                                                                            | CDRRMO / OCD VIII            |\n| Organisational structure   | Reconcile JMC 2014-1, approved plantilla and proposed CDRRMO department structure.                                                                                              | HRMO / CDRRMO                |\n| Institutional maturity     | Validate provisional scores and resolve contradictory evidence.                                                                                                                 | Assessment team / CDRRMO     |\n| Local issuances            | Obtain the missing issuance years (Table 1.9) or record them as not obtained; reconcile Ordinance No. 37, s. 1993 high-risk areas with current hazard maps.                     | CDRRMO Secretariat / CPDO    |\n| Version control            | The workbook is now v1-7 (updated 11 November 2026), while its Guide sheet still identifies the instrument as Version 1.1. Reconcile the workbook version and revision history. | Assessment team              |\n\nTwo important corrections incorporated into this revised chapter:\n\n1.  A State of Calamity is declared by the local sanggunian upon CDRRMC recommendation, not simply through a Mayor’s Executive Order.\n\n2.  A State of Imminent Disaster is a separate anticipatory mechanism under RA 12287. The Local Chief Executive may declare it through an Executive Order upon the prescribed Regional DRRM Council recommendation.\n\nThese distinctions are essential to legal compliance.\n\n## 16. Relationship With the Succeeding Chapters\n\nThe Manual has ten chapters (Introduction, section 4). To avoid duplication, each subject is owned by one chapter:\n\n**Table 1.27**\n\n*Chapters of the Manual and Their Principal Assessment References*\n\n| **Ch.** | **Title**                                                                 | **Principal Assessment References**             |\n|---------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| 1       | Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability           | A1, A10.5, B1.1, Part C governance              |\n| 2       | CDRRMO Organisational Structure, Staffing and Human Resource Management   | A2, B4, B2 (EOC facility and continuity assets) |\n| 3       | Risk Intelligence, Planning and Institutional Preparedness                | A3, A5                                          |\n| 4       | Early Warning, EOC Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response | A6, A7, A8, B1.2–B1.5, B5                       |\n| 5       | Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better                            | A9, A8.7, A10.6                                 |\n| 6       | DRRM Financing, Resource Management and Accountability                    | A4, A7.5                                        |\n| 7       | Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement   | A10.3–A10.9, A5.4                               |\n| 8       | Stakeholder Engagement and Institutional Partnerships                     | A10.1–A10.2, Part C                             |\n| 9       | Public Awareness, Community Resilience and Capacity Development           | A6.4–A6.6, A7, A2.6                             |\n| 10      | Institutional Sustainability, Deep Resilience and Recognition             | A10.7, B3.3, B3.5                               |\n\n*Note. Structure adopted on 12 November 2026. The contractual functional modules, the ten functional areas and this chapter sequence are different levels of one Manual.*\n\n## 17. Concluding Notes\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal, institutional and governance foundation for the integrated operation of Ormoc City’s CDRRMC, CDRRMO and EOC.\n\nIt recognises that urban resilience is not produced by a single office or emergency facility. It emerges from the effective interaction of executive leadership, institutional capacity, policy coordination, risk information, financial governance, community participation and operational systems.\n\nThe institutional assessment demonstrates that Ormoc has established important DRRM governance practices, particularly regular CDRRMC meetings, Secretariat functions, DRRM representation in development and financial bodies, and existing planning and partnership mechanisms. However, gaps in legal institutionalisation, EOC authority, anticipatory governance and cross-office coordination continue to constrain the effectiveness of the wider system.\n\nThe provisions of this chapter respond to these issues by establishing:\n\n- Clear institutional mandates and decision rights;\n\n- Defined process ownership and responsibility assignments;\n\n- Standardised governance workflows;\n\n- Legally differentiated declaration procedures;\n\n- Formalised institutional and inter-agency interfaces;\n\n- RACI-based accountability;\n\n- TQM and PDCA-based quality assurance; and\n\n- Evidence-based monitoring and continuous improvement.\n\nThe ultimate objective is to move Ormoc City’s DRRM governance from arrangements that are partly dependent on individual initiative toward a legally grounded, process-driven, integrated and continuously improving institutional system.\n\nThis foundation shall support the implementation of the succeeding modules, particularly organisational management, preparedness, EOC operations, information management, resource mobilisation, recovery and performance improvement.\n\n## References\n\nThe following references provide the legal and technical foundation for this chapter. The final bibliography should be consolidated with the complete MOO reference register.\n\n- City Government of Ormoc, City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office. (2026). *CDRRMO reference: Local policies* \\[Shared Drive folder of the City’s DRRM policy instruments: Sangguniang Panlungsod ordinances, executive orders, CDRRMC resolutions and SP resolutions\\]. https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/12NWwUTAE6ub1vW31M2NSMktYlP-BP4oR\n\n- City Government of Ormoc, Sangguniang Panlungsod. (1993–2025). *SP ordinances* \\[Shared Drive folder, by year, including Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 establishing the CDRRMO and Ordinance No. 011, s. 2015 amending it\\]. https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/132g-VeSpXkorcNCte2fpyVY3Az3baBXS\n\n- City Government of Ormoc. (2026). Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool (v1-7, 11 November 2026). Unpublished assessment workbook.\n\n- City Government of Ormoc, City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office. (2026). CDRRMO organisational chart and internal staff papers on planning, budgeting and cluster-based barangay coordination. Unpublished working papers.\n\n- National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2018). Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018: Guidelines on the establishment, operationalisation and management of emergency operations centers.\n\n- City Government of Ormoc, City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2021–2025). CDRRMC resolutions, including Res. Nos. 01–12, 2-A, 2-B, 14 and 15 s.2021; 01–09 s.2022; 01–07 s.2023; 01–06, 09 and 10 s.2024; 01–08 s.2025.\n\n- City Government of Ormoc, Office of the City Mayor. (2022–2024). Executive Order Nos. 2022-0006, 2022-00020, 2023-0041 and 2024-032A.\n\n- Sangguniang Panlungsod ng Ormoc. (2021). Resolution Nos. 2021-036, 2021-149, 2021-357 and 2021-365 to 2021-369.\n\n- Department of Budget and Management, Department of the Interior and Local Government, Civil Service Commission, & National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2014). Joint Memorandum Circular No. 2014-1: Implementing guidelines for the establishment of Local Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Offices or Barangay Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Committees in Local Government Units. Official DBM issuance.\n\n- National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2012). Memorandum Circular No. 4, s. 2012: Implementing guidelines on the use of the Incident Command System.\n\n- National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2026). Implementing Rules and Regulations of Republic Act No. 12287. Signed September 14, 2026, as identified in the assessment workbook.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (1991). Republic Act No. 7160: Local Government Code of 1991.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2009). Republic Act No. 9729: Climate Change Act of 2009.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2010). Republic Act No. 10121: Philippine Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Act of 2010. Full text of RA 10121.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2012). Republic Act No. 10173: Data Privacy Act of 2012.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2018). Republic Act No. 11036: Mental Health Act.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2024). Republic Act No. 12076: Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2024). Republic Act No. 12009: New Government Procurement Act.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2025). Republic Act No. 12287: Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act. Official legislative reference.\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2015). Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030.\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2017). Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities: Detailed level assessment.\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2018). Making cities resilient 2030: Ten Essentials for Making Cities Resilient.\n\n- International Organization for Standardization. (2015). ISO 9001:2015: Quality management systems—Requirements.\n\n- International Organization for Standardization. (2018). ISO 31000:2018: Risk management—Guidelines.\n\n- International Organization for Standardization. (2019). ISO 22301:2019: Security and resilience—Business continuity management systems—Requirements.\n\n## Institutional Implementation Package\n\nThis package gives operational effect to Chapter 1 through a coordinated set of proposed legislative and executive instruments, standard operating procedures, responsibility matrices and governance templates. It is an integrated annex set and must be read with Chapter 1, particularly Sections 6–8, 11–14.\n\n## Annex Register and Cross-Reference Guide\n\n**Table 1.28**\n\n*Annex Register and Cross-Reference Guide*\n\n| **Annex** | **Instrument**                                                                                              | **Approval / Control Route**           |\n|-----------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|\n| 1-A       | Proposed CDRRMO Institutionalisation Ordinance (to be recast as an amendment of Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014) | Sangguniang Panlungsod                 |\n| 1-B       | Proposed CDRRMC Reconstitution Executive Order                                                              | City Mayor                             |\n| 1-C       | Proposed EOC Establishment Executive Order                                                                  | City Mayor                             |\n| 1-D       | SOP GOV-04: State of Calamity Declaration and Lifting                                                       | Approval under City delegation         |\n| 1-E       | SOP GOV-05: State of Imminent Disaster / Anticipatory Action                                                | Approval after RA 12287 IRR validation |\n| 1-F       | Master RACI and Institutional Interface Matrix                                                              | CDRRMC / City approval                 |\n| 1-G       | Governance Forms GOV-F01 to GOV-F16                                                                         | CDRRMO Secretariat custodian           |\n| 1-H       | Implementation Roadmap                                                                                      | City Mayor                             |\n\n**Consistent definitions**. CDRRMC means City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council; CDRRMO means permanent City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office;\n\n**Annex 1-I** (added 12 November 2026) is the Register of Local DRRM Issuances that follows.\n\n## Annex 1-I. Register of Local DRRM Issuances\n\nThis annex lists every local DRRM issuance obtained as of 12 November 2026, grouped by type and ordered by year. It is the printed form of the Vault register; the Vault copy, with links to each scan, is the controlled register.\n\n**Table 1.29**\n\n*Register of Local DRRM Issuances, 1993–2025*\n\n| **No.** | **Instrument**                                | **Date**    | **Subject**                                                                                                                                                                                             | **IAT Links**                 |\n|---------|----------